<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; padding: 15px 0; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="color: white; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Future Careers – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: white; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Future Careers</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Want to Be a Doctor Because...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Explain a recent learning experience. Interactive: Engage in questions and answers about future goals.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can explain a past learning experience relevant to a future career by answering questions and sharing reflections (e.g., "What did you learn during your presentation?" "I learned to speak clearly and organize my ideas because I want to be a teacher.").
Can ask and answer questions about personal career goals (e.g., "What are your plans for the future?" "I want to be a flight attendant.").
Can respond to questions about personal goals related to specific professions (e.g., "What do you need to do to become a flight attendant?" "I need to study languages and have good communication skills.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by showing pictures of various professionals (e.g., a doctor, a lawyer, a teacher, an engineer, a chef). Students briefly discuss in pairs: "What do these people do?" and "Which of these careers is important?" (2-3 minutes).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences about future career goals: "I want to be a teacher because I like helping students learn." The teacher then points to a picture of a doctor and says, "Maybe someone wants to be a doctor. What will they need to do?" Teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of modals "would" and "will" in context, focusing on the /ʊ/ and /ɪ/ sounds. Teacher also models asking "What do you want to be?" and "Why do you want to be a [career]?"</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I want to be a doctor,' am I talking about now or the future?" (Future)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I already have the job, or is it a wish/intention?" (A wish/intention)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do you need to do to achieve your goals?" (Study, train, prepare, etc.)</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Future Careers"</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio dialogue (2-3 minutes) between two 8th-grade students discussing their future careers after high school. One student wants to be a "lawyer" and the other a "doctor." The dialogue includes phrases like "What are your plans for the future?" "I would like to be a lawyer because..." "I will need to study hard," "What about you? What do you want to be?" and "I want to be a doctor to help people."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will understand spoken descriptions of future career intentions and the reasons behind them.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions (after first listening):</b> "Who is talking?" "What are they talking about?" "What careers do they mention?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (after second listening):</b> Students answer T/F questions based on the dialogue. E.g., "The girl wants to be a teacher." (False) "The boy wants to help people as a doctor." (True) "To become a lawyer, she needs to study languages." (False, she needs to study law).</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-10 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Speaking</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Practicing Wh-questions for career goals, future tense for plans ("I will need to..."), modals for intention and suggestions ("I would like to..."), "want to + infinitive verb." Vocabulary: doctor, lawyer, teacher, career, goals, plan, prepare, professional, skilled, important. Pronunciation practice of modals (/ʊ/, /ɪ/) and minimal pairs (/ʃ/ vs /tʃ/, /dʒ/ vs /tʃ/) within career-related words (e.g., "profes<b>sion</b>al" /ʃ/ vs "<b>ch</b>allenge" /tʃ/, "<b>j</b>ob" /dʒ/ vs "<b>ch</b>oice" /tʃ/).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Sentence Scramble and Completion (Individual/Choral):</b> The teacher provides sentence fragments on the board or projector. Students orally complete them using target vocabulary and grammar, focusing on clear pronunciation.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "I want to be a _______ because I like helping others." (teacher/doctor)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "To achieve my goals, I will need to _______." (study/train/prepare)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "What do you _______ to be in the future?" (want)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher drills pronunciation of "should," "would," "will" and minimal pairs relevant to career words (e.g., "ship" for "scholarship" vs. "chip" for "challenge").</li>
                    </ul></li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice - Question and Answer Chain Drill (Whole Class/Groups):</b> The teacher starts a chain by asking a student, "What do you want to be in the future?" The student answers, "I want to be a [career] because..." and then asks the next student, "What do you plan to do after high school?" This continues around the class or within small groups. Teacher monitors for correct grammar, vocabulary, and pronunciation of modals.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - "Career Qualities" Match (Pair work):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides cards with different careers (doctor, lawyer, teacher) and cards with corresponding qualities or actions (e.g., "needs good communication skills," "studies law," "helps sick people," "needs professional training"). Students orally match the careers to the qualities, explaining their choices: "A doctor needs to be skilled in medicine." "To be a lawyer, you would need to study law." They practice asking: "What qualifications do you need for this career?"</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-20 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Future Careers"</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Career Goal Interview" - Students work in pairs to role-play a short interview or discussion about their future career goals. One student is the "interviewer" (e.g., a guidance counselor or a friend), and the other is the "student" explaining their aspirations.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive a role card and a few guiding questions/prompts.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interviewer's prompts:</b> "What are your plans for the future?" "What do you want to be?" "Why do you want to be a [career]?" "What do you need to do to become one?" "What specific goals do you have for achieving this?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student's prompts:</b> "I want to be a [doctor/lawyer/teacher] because I [like helping people/love justice/enjoy teaching]." "I would like to [study medicine/go to law school/get professional qualifications]." "I will need to [train successfully/prepare well/achieve good grades]."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students engage in a conversation of 2-3 minutes per pair, rotating roles after the first round.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher circulates, listening for active use of target grammar (Wh-questions, 'want to + infinitive,' future tense, modals) and vocabulary, as well as clarity and interaction.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Encourage sociolinguistic interaction: students providing suggestions for improvement in their partner's plan or asking follow-up questions. E.g., "That's a great plan! Have you thought about an internship?"</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment strategy</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each pair briefly presents their partner's career goals and plans to the class (1 minute per pair). For example, "My partner, [Name], wants to be a [career] because [reason]. She/He will need to [action 1] and [action 2]."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist & Peer Assessment):</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of modals (should, would, will) and differentiation of minimal pairs (e.g., /ʃ/ in "professional," /tʃ/ in "achieve").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Accurately uses words like doctor, lawyer, teacher, career, goals, plan, train, achieve, professional, skilled.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target grammar:</b> Correctly uses Wh-questions for career goals, "want to + infinitive," future tense ("I will need to..."), and modals for intention ("I would like to...").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of explanation:</b> Explains career choice and plans clearly and logically.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration and interaction:</b> Actively listens, asks relevant questions, and responds appropriately.</li>
            </ul></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief exit ticket with the following prompts:
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word or phrase I used today to talk about future careers is..."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The most important thing I learned about explaining career goals is..."</li>
            </ul></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-8 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of today's speaking activity was easy for me?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging when talking about my future career plans, and what can I do to improve?"</li>
            </ul></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in terms of student engagement and language production? Did students successfully use the target grammar and vocabulary in their explanations and interactions? Were the pronunciation points effectively integrated? What adjustments or additional scaffolds (e.g., more sentence frames, specific prompt cards) might be needed for students struggling to express their ideas orally?</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Today we practiced speaking about our future career plans and reasons. Next time, we will use these ideas to write a short paragraph about our 'dream career' and the steps we need to take to achieve it." (Lesson 4: Writing Composition)</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
    </tr>
</table>